00:24:31.970 --> 00:24:35.420
about Ian and I so our main
focus isn't let's go do a movie,

00:24:35.420 --> 00:24:38.780
let's go be huge Hollywood
hotshots or whatever.

00:24:38.780 --> 00:24:43.220
Let's grow the brand and
keep our comedy alive

00:24:43.220 --> 00:24:45.860
in as many different forms as we can.

00:24:45.860 --> 00:24:48.586
We have our gaming channel,
we have a cartoon channel.

00:24:48.586 --> 00:24:50.460
We're trying to introduce
new personalities

00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:51.950
that totally get our humor

00:24:51.950 --> 00:24:54.583
that we think our audience will like.

00:24:54.583 --> 00:24:56.940
They love all the Smosh games guys now

00:24:56.940 --> 00:25:00.880
and they love Mari so those
have been some personalities

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:03.491
that we've introduced that totally get it.

00:25:03.491 --> 00:25:06.480
So more of that type of thing too,

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:08.310
other Smosh properties.

00:25:08.310 --> 00:25:09.900
Yeah, other people that we really love

00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:12.400
that we know that our
audience will totally get.

00:25:12.400 --> 00:25:15.905
Yeah 'cause we can't
create 50 shows ourselves

00:25:15.905 --> 00:25:20.520
but we can create other
shows that other people make

00:25:20.520 --> 00:25:22.520
that our audience can enjoy just as much

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:24.270
as they enjoy watching our shows.

00:25:24.270 --> 00:25:27.070
I think that's cool.

00:25:27.070 --> 00:25:28.450
Guess I just need to say thanks

00:25:28.450 --> 00:25:30.188
for all that strategic development.

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:35.730
They turned a corner
at that point in terms

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:39.829
of casting, expanding the Smosh cast

00:25:39.829 --> 00:25:42.770
to include everyone that
we know very well now

00:25:42.770 --> 00:25:44.470
because they work in our building.

00:25:46.586 --> 00:25:51.586
And then Anthony and Ian,
they separated creatively

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:56.090
and if you listen back to that podcast,

00:25:56.090 --> 00:26:01.070
I think in hindsight you
can probably read into it

00:26:01.070 --> 00:26:03.250
maybe more than was actually there,

00:26:03.250 --> 00:26:06.810
but that's a valid
exercise if you wanna do it

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:10.870
to see if you wanna over-interpret
some creative tension

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:15.870
or just creative trajectory
being kinda splitting up.

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:19.190
And that was still pretty early in 2014,

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:23.093
it would be another, what, four years,

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:26.860
before Anthony left.

00:26:26.860 --> 00:26:31.530
I don't know, if I would
have known that we would have

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:35.530
acquired Smosh and brought them in-house,

00:26:35.530 --> 00:26:40.530
and given Ian the freedom
with his team to continue

00:26:41.120 --> 00:26:45.160
to create as they have, I just
don't know how I would feel

00:26:45.160 --> 00:26:47.740
doing that interview,
I'd probably be like,

00:26:47.740 --> 00:26:50.240
that's why I can't be a time traveler.

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:52.330
'Cause I'd have this
weird grin on my face,

00:26:52.330 --> 00:26:53.670
they'd be like what's wrong with you, man?

00:26:53.670 --> 00:26:56.910
Well we had no point of reference.

00:26:56.910 --> 00:27:01.910
Absolutely, I mean, I would
say that up until the moment

00:27:02.170 --> 00:27:04.550
that it started to seem
like it could happen,

00:27:04.550 --> 00:27:08.273
we had no point of
reference for acquiring.

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:11.660
You know what I'm saying,
that wasn't really,

00:27:11.660 --> 00:27:14.080
it wasn't really on our
minds, it wasn't part

00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:15.233
of the strategic plan.

00:27:16.560 --> 00:27:21.450
But yeah, 'cause we've looked up to Smosh

00:27:21.450 --> 00:27:22.610
for a really long time.

00:27:22.610 --> 00:27:26.333
You go back to the very beginning.

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:29.850
Probably when we started
our YouTube channel,

00:27:31.020 --> 00:27:33.340
they had to be number
one, it was either them

00:27:33.340 --> 00:27:34.610
or Barats and Bereta.

00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:36.810
No, they were number one.

00:27:36.810 --> 00:27:41.290
Historically they are in the top five,

00:27:41.290 --> 00:27:44.580
maybe the top three most
subscribed channels on YouTube

00:27:45.460 --> 00:27:46.980
in terms of longevity.

00:27:46.980 --> 00:27:49.320
Obviously holding that
title for a very long time.

00:27:49.320 --> 00:27:52.990
Yeah, obviously PewDiePie
is up at the top.

00:27:52.990 --> 00:27:55.330
Which we also have an interview with him.

00:27:55.330 --> 00:27:58.967
We don't have a clip of
talking with Felix but--

00:27:58.967 --> 00:28:01.670
But you talked about another
clip we're not gonna play

00:28:01.670 --> 00:28:06.640
but I wanna talk about it 'cause
it is the Wassabi Brothers.

00:28:06.640 --> 00:28:09.030
We had Wassabi Brothers on.
Yeah.

00:28:09.030 --> 00:28:13.250
And we actually, whenever
we have a duo on,

00:28:13.250 --> 00:28:15.770
we talk about their--
Creative dynamic,

00:28:15.770 --> 00:28:17.640
relational dynamic.
All that stuff.

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:21.500
And just, you know,
from one duo to another,

00:28:21.500 --> 00:28:24.570
let's talk about that and
how you work through things.

00:28:24.570 --> 00:28:27.120
And when we started asking those questions

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:29.860
about how you work through
things and differences,

00:28:29.860 --> 00:28:32.700
et cetera, with the Wassabi Bros,

00:28:32.700 --> 00:28:34.940
it became very evident
to us and you can go back

00:28:34.940 --> 00:28:39.210
and listen to the episode
that this didn't seem like

00:28:39.210 --> 00:28:40.910
something that was gonna last.

00:28:40.910 --> 00:28:45.123
And I would say that within,
it was less than a year,

00:28:46.060 --> 00:28:47.620
might have been a few months.

00:28:47.620 --> 00:28:48.470
Right.

00:28:48.470 --> 00:28:50.320
They announced that they were separating.

00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:51.760
Roi and Alex.

00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:55.090
Roi's gonna do a bunch
of bathing and stuff.

00:28:55.090 --> 00:28:56.530
Yeah, they both gone on

00:28:56.530 --> 00:28:58.280
to be very successful individually.

00:29:02.320 --> 00:29:07.320
It didn't seem like they had
necessarily the commitment

00:29:07.380 --> 00:29:11.660
to be like this is us
doing this together or bust

00:29:11.660 --> 00:29:15.850
and that usually is required
and even when you do have

00:29:15.850 --> 00:29:17.960
that commitment sometimes
it doesn't work out.

00:29:17.960 --> 00:29:21.429
Yeah I think that was April 30th, 2018

00:29:21.429 --> 00:29:24.475
when that finally came to an end.

00:29:24.475 --> 00:29:25.883
Like Link was saying,

00:29:26.790 --> 00:29:31.790
the majority of the episodes
early on were these interviews

00:29:31.954 --> 00:29:36.660
because that was the, we
probably have said this before

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:39.210
but we were really
influenced by Marc Maron,

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:41.767
WTF Podcasts at the time

00:29:41.767 --> 00:29:44.260
and that he was interviewing
these traditional celebrities

00:29:44.260 --> 00:29:47.000
and getting into very personal things

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:52.000
and this wasn't something
we had any experience with.

00:29:52.530 --> 00:29:57.530
Also it's a little bit
odd, there's an odd dynamic

00:29:58.240 --> 00:30:02.750
when two guys are interviewing
one person typically

00:30:02.750 --> 00:30:07.290
on a personal level and so
it's kinda like, ah okay,

00:30:07.290 --> 00:30:10.240
this is an interesting dynamic.

00:30:10.240 --> 00:30:12.690
Probably don't need two
people to ask a question,

00:30:14.740 --> 00:30:17.540
interview somebody where
75 to 80% of the content

00:30:17.540 --> 00:30:20.530
is gonna be the answers
that this person is giving.

00:30:20.530 --> 00:30:23.520
I mean, there was some cool
things that happened with us,

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:25.210
kinda bouncing things off of each other.

00:30:25.210 --> 00:30:28.200
There's two different
perspectives applied to it.

00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:31.160
But we both have a lot to say.

00:30:31.160 --> 00:30:32.630
You know what I'm saying?

00:30:32.630 --> 00:30:35.640
And so every once in
awhile, even back then,

00:30:35.640 --> 00:30:38.410
we would work in one of these
episodes where it was just,

00:30:38.410 --> 00:30:40.080
hey let's just talk to each other.

00:30:40.080 --> 00:30:43.810
But when we decided to do the podcast,

00:30:43.810 --> 00:30:45.430
we immediately went to the interview thing

00:30:45.430 --> 00:30:47.690
because there were so
many people doing that,

00:30:47.690 --> 00:30:49.280
especially in the comedy
world like you said

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:53.600
but no one was doing it in that deep,

00:30:55.413 --> 00:31:00.413
kinda introspective, analytical, raw way

00:31:00.810 --> 00:31:02.310
with YouTubers.
YouTubers.

00:31:02.310 --> 00:31:06.463
So I was very excited
about that in terms of,

00:31:07.370 --> 00:31:11.504
actually I felt like it was
collecting YouTuber interviews

00:31:11.504 --> 00:31:15.770
as a time capsule and I'm
very proud of the interviews

00:31:15.770 --> 00:31:19.808
that we did because I
do think it represented

00:31:19.808 --> 00:31:22.480
everyone's story up into that point,

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:25.990
how they got to that point in being

00:31:25.990 --> 00:31:27.363
so successful on YouTube,

00:31:28.556 --> 00:31:30.790
and I do think it's
useful to still go back

00:31:30.790 --> 00:31:32.980
and see that YouTube has changed so much

00:31:32.980 --> 00:31:37.685
that a lot of the success
paths that they took

00:31:37.685 --> 00:31:40.450
are no longer viable paths because

00:31:40.450 --> 00:31:43.093
YouTube is a different
thing, it works differently.

00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:46.590
Everything's different about everything.

00:31:46.590 --> 00:31:50.313
But I still think it's a time capsule.

00:31:51.390 --> 00:31:52.800
I know for the Felix interview,

00:31:52.800 --> 00:31:57.430
it was very obvious to us
that he had never really,

00:31:57.430 --> 00:31:59.020
to the level that we
were trying to get him

00:31:59.020 --> 00:32:01.727
to analyze his own success,
really grappled with that.

00:32:01.727 --> 00:32:05.440
And it was a combination
of somebody who was willing

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:08.670
to ask those kinds of questions
but also had the insight

00:32:08.670 --> 00:32:12.460
into what they were doing because
it was what we were doing.

00:32:12.460 --> 00:32:15.040
It's the reason that Marc
Maron's podcast works so well

00:32:15.040 --> 00:32:17.290
is he's talking to
comedians and performers

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:19.820
and he's a comedian and performer.

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:22.583
Yeah and I think it
had to do with our age.

00:32:23.870 --> 00:32:27.750
I think we were already old
in terms of the ecosystem

00:32:27.750 --> 00:32:31.470
of YouTube and so, and we
had been doing it for awhile.

00:32:31.470 --> 00:32:35.060
We knew a lot of these people
and they respected us enough

00:32:35.060 --> 00:32:36.560
to say yeah, we'll come in and talk to you

00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:39.250
and it took on a life of its own because

00:32:40.953 --> 00:32:44.166
a guest-driven podcast works differently

00:32:44.166 --> 00:32:49.166
than a talent-driven, I'm
using the term talent loosely,

00:32:50.170 --> 00:32:52.120
where it's just two
guys who are gonna talk

00:32:52.120 --> 00:32:55.290
to each other every single,
host-driven, you know.

00:32:55.290 --> 00:32:57.200
It works differently
and it draws people in

00:32:57.200 --> 00:32:58.980
in a different way so there
are people who are coming

00:32:58.980 --> 00:33:03.450
into an Ear Biscuits
podcast because of the guest

00:33:03.450 --> 00:33:08.070
and we kinda relied on that for, you know,

00:33:08.070 --> 00:33:10.410
basically the entire
first and second season.

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:14.160
Yeah but like you said, well,

00:33:14.160 --> 00:33:15.130
and I do wanna add one thing.

00:33:15.130 --> 00:33:18.860
I do think that, there was
a question in our minds

00:33:18.860 --> 00:33:20.960
I think when we first came up with

00:33:20.960 --> 00:33:23.363
what Ear Biscuits was gonna be that,

00:33:25.850 --> 00:33:27.660
do people wanna hear us talk for an hour?

00:33:27.660 --> 00:33:29.403
Do we wanna talk for an hour?

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:36.670
We moved to the interview thing so quickly

00:33:36.670 --> 00:33:40.560
that I just wonder if
we questioned whether

00:33:40.560 --> 00:33:43.540
what we're doing now
where it's just the two

00:33:43.540 --> 00:33:45.963
