00:38:47.730 --> 00:38:48.697
I think that--

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:50.620
That's one you can fit in.

00:38:50.620 --> 00:38:55.170
Getting, writing the novel,
looking for some inspiration

00:38:55.170 --> 00:38:59.740
and then continuing to think
about where we could go

00:38:59.740 --> 00:39:00.853
with that story.

00:39:03.330 --> 00:39:05.940
I'd say that's the latest
thing is I'm consuming

00:39:05.940 --> 00:39:10.540
a lot of thriller slash
horror novels but in terms

00:39:10.540 --> 00:39:15.310
of things that require
buying equipment and,

00:39:15.310 --> 00:39:18.950
I anticipate getting
more back into the art

00:39:18.950 --> 00:39:23.100
of barbecue when we re-do the
outside and I've got my eye

00:39:23.100 --> 00:39:26.340
on this grill that I want or whatever,

00:39:26.340 --> 00:39:31.340
but it's not like, last
time I smoked a pork butt

00:39:31.616 --> 00:39:34.921
for a large group of people, again,

00:39:34.921 --> 00:39:38.600
I gotta plan it, I gotta
get ready the night before.

00:39:38.600 --> 00:39:41.650
I gotta spend six to eight
hours on the day of like--

00:39:41.650 --> 00:39:43.540
But at least you're home so that's doable

00:39:43.540 --> 00:39:45.570
and you can still be a dad.

00:39:45.570 --> 00:39:47.870
No but I can't sit there
with a piece of pork

00:39:47.870 --> 00:39:50.080
if I gotta go to a basketball
game or take somebody

00:39:50.080 --> 00:39:51.828
to a birthday party.
Yeah that's true.

00:39:51.828 --> 00:39:54.146
You know what I'm saying,
you gotta plan these things.

00:39:54.146 --> 00:39:57.050
Those episodes where we would just talk

00:39:57.050 --> 00:39:59.620
the two of us and kind of share stories

00:39:59.620 --> 00:40:01.470
and talk about our songwriting process

00:40:01.470 --> 00:40:04.413
or specific dynamics of our friendship,

00:40:06.020 --> 00:40:11.020
I think were rays of light of shining away

00:40:11.800 --> 00:40:15.450
to where Ear Biscuits
could ultimately evolve.

00:40:15.450 --> 00:40:17.410
And we were getting good
feedback on those as well

00:40:17.410 --> 00:40:18.600
from you guys.

00:40:18.600 --> 00:40:22.463
And I think that you had
a mounting frustration

00:40:25.880 --> 00:40:28.823
with the interviews, more so than me.

00:40:30.700 --> 00:40:32.013
Which is totally fine.

00:40:33.408 --> 00:40:36.053
I think it got to a
point where it was like,

00:40:37.520 --> 00:40:41.030
I was a little later kinda
making that transition

00:40:41.030 --> 00:40:43.260
but ultimately we both
got to the point where

00:40:43.260 --> 00:40:45.650
we didn't really have an appetite

00:40:45.650 --> 00:40:48.523
for these in-depth interviews.

00:40:49.447 --> 00:40:52.627
Especially when our appetite
for having more conversations

00:40:52.627 --> 00:40:54.720
started to grow, we can get into

00:40:54.720 --> 00:40:57.710
that a little bit more
later but I think as far

00:40:57.710 --> 00:41:02.020
as the waning interest in interviews,

00:41:03.290 --> 00:41:05.309
part of it was practical.

00:41:05.309 --> 00:41:08.470
You're subject to these
people, guests showing up,

00:41:08.470 --> 00:41:11.750
having to be booked,
having to pick people.

00:41:11.750 --> 00:41:13.220
Are they the right kinda candidate,

00:41:13.220 --> 00:41:15.557
do we wanna talk with 'em,
do we have things to say?

00:41:15.557 --> 00:41:18.330
It was a whole thing that was very--

00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:21.550
And for me personally, I was,

00:41:21.550 --> 00:41:23.690
there were some guests that I was

00:41:23.690 --> 00:41:26.170
personally less motivated to talk to.

00:41:26.170 --> 00:41:30.400
I was personally less
interested in their career,

00:41:30.400 --> 00:41:31.700
just being frank about it.

00:41:32.630 --> 00:41:34.760
I respected everybody and I learned a lot.

00:41:34.760 --> 00:41:37.340
I would talk, we'd sometimes
have people and I was like,

00:41:37.340 --> 00:41:40.377
I really don't know
anything about what you do,

00:41:40.377 --> 00:41:44.770
but I'm gonna take, we
would have a research period

00:41:44.770 --> 00:41:47.050
so we wanted to ask informed questions

00:41:47.050 --> 00:41:49.150
and we wanted to know
what we were talking about

00:41:49.150 --> 00:41:52.500
and so there would be time
that would go into learning

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:55.650
about them, watching their
videos and then coming up

00:41:55.650 --> 00:41:59.890
with some semi-intelligent questions,

00:41:59.890 --> 00:42:04.740
and that process became a
little more like homework to me

00:42:04.740 --> 00:42:06.940
about a subject that I
wasn't interested in.

00:42:08.740 --> 00:42:11.805
And I think that just for me,

00:42:11.805 --> 00:42:14.390
and I think you couple
that with the fact that,

00:42:14.390 --> 00:42:18.510
like I said, we both have a lot to say.

00:42:18.510 --> 00:42:23.510
We can fill conversation
very, very easily,

00:42:24.120 --> 00:42:25.030
both of us.

00:42:25.030 --> 00:42:28.659
Both of us individually
have enough conversation

00:42:28.659 --> 00:42:32.540
in the tank to talk to one person.

00:42:32.540 --> 00:42:34.500
When all of a sudden you got the two of us

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:37.033
and sometimes there was, there was this,

00:42:37.893 --> 00:42:40.130
and I think you had more
of a tendency to do this

00:42:40.130 --> 00:42:42.770
to be like, you had this very specific,

00:42:42.770 --> 00:42:44.237
and we wouldn't talk about it.

00:42:44.237 --> 00:42:45.760
You had a very specific
way that you wanted

00:42:45.760 --> 00:42:47.670
to conduct the interview
and a specific line

00:42:47.670 --> 00:42:50.440
of questioning and so there would be like

00:42:50.440 --> 00:42:52.770
maybe a 40 minute period in a podcast

00:42:52.770 --> 00:42:55.363
where I might just say like yeah.

00:42:56.580 --> 00:43:00.910
And you know me, that's not my jam.

00:43:00.910 --> 00:43:04.428
And so, that was frustrating for me.

00:43:04.428 --> 00:43:06.880
Yeah.
'Cause I was like,

00:43:06.880 --> 00:43:08.230
let's just talk, man.

00:43:08.230 --> 00:43:09.383
We got plenty to say.

00:43:10.370 --> 00:43:13.719
Yeah, you know what I think I'm realizing

00:43:13.719 --> 00:43:16.580
or at least remembering, one of the things

00:43:16.580 --> 00:43:19.790
that really changed for
me in terms of my appetite

00:43:19.790 --> 00:43:24.750
for wanting to switch the show
and make it more about us,

00:43:24.750 --> 00:43:29.750
I had this mounting guilt,
maybe guilt's a strong word

00:43:30.240 --> 00:43:34.150
but I just, I had a mounting discomfort

00:43:34.150 --> 00:43:38.416
over the course of a couple of
years of doing the interviews

00:43:38.416 --> 00:43:43.416
because I would make it a
practice to push so hard

00:43:44.720 --> 00:43:49.720
to get to something juicy, to
get to something meaningful

00:43:50.400 --> 00:43:52.223
and introspective and new.

00:43:53.427 --> 00:43:56.960
I took it as a personal
challenge to get past

00:43:56.960 --> 00:43:59.059
what they would talk
about on other interviews

00:43:59.059 --> 00:44:03.480
and really understand not only
how they became successful

00:44:03.480 --> 00:44:07.210
but what made them tick.

00:44:07.210 --> 00:44:11.240
If they went through any
hardship, it felt important to me

00:44:11.240 --> 00:44:15.460
to give them an opportunity
to talk about that

00:44:15.460 --> 00:44:20.460
because I just felt like
that's an important aspect

00:44:20.652 --> 00:44:22.420
that a lot of people didn't see.

00:44:22.420 --> 00:44:27.420
They see the success, they
see the smiles in between,

00:44:27.520 --> 00:44:29.303
jump cut together.

00:44:29.303 --> 00:44:31.929
But they don't see in
between the jump cuts,

00:44:31.929 --> 00:44:36.929
and I really wanted to
give people an opportunity

00:44:37.430 --> 00:44:42.430
to analyze that aspect
of their own selves,

00:44:45.939 --> 00:44:50.939
and I did push kinda hard,
I asked awkward questions.

00:44:54.718 --> 00:44:57.417
And that's actually not what bothered me,

00:44:57.417 --> 00:45:00.200
but I think the mounting discomfort was--

00:45:00.200 --> 00:45:02.680
We weren't doing it.
We weren't doing that.

00:45:02.680 --> 00:45:03.513
We weren't sharing.

00:45:03.513 --> 00:45:06.300
I think we started talking
about it, so I felt like,

00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:09.130
if someone would have done
to me what I did to them

00:45:09.130 --> 00:45:11.810
in the line of questioning
that I presented,

00:45:11.810 --> 00:45:14.920
I would have been very uncomfortable

00:45:14.920 --> 00:45:16.980
and I didn't feel like that was fair.

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:19.663
I still don't feel like
that was totally fair.

00:45:21.984 --> 00:45:26.984
But I'm glad that this
venue and this table

00:45:28.280 --> 00:45:33.280
has evolved to be a space
where we can process who we are

00:45:33.926 --> 00:45:37.400
in a way that I was trying
to get other people to do.

00:45:37.400 --> 00:45:42.400
And I think that, I definitely
recall people commenting

00:45:44.600 --> 00:45:47.980
about that, like you guys will get so,

00:45:47.980 --> 00:45:51.140
so much personal stuff from a guest

00:45:51.140 --> 00:45:53.390
but we don't know a lot
about you personally.

00:45:53.390 --> 00:45:56.690
Right, I'll play a clip.

00:45:56.690 --> 00:45:58.173
I didn't mean to cut you off,
you can finish your thought.

00:45:58.173 --> 00:46:00.050
That was all I was gonna say.

00:46:00.050 --> 00:46:03.280
So episode 91, actually we
called it Let's Get Personal,

00:46:03.280 --> 00:46:05.740
released on April 24th, 2017.

00:46:05.740 --> 00:46:08.590
Speaking of Lily, we took her
in for just an annual checkup.

00:46:08.590 --> 00:46:10.590
They had concerns, sent
her to a specialist

00:46:10.590 --> 00:46:12.420
and then to make a very long story short,

00:46:12.420 --> 00:46:15.670
she was diagnosed with severe scoliosis.

00:46:15.670 --> 00:46:18.180
We get the x-ray, we look at this thing

00:46:18.180 --> 00:46:20.570
and her back is like an S.

00:46:20.570 --> 00:46:22.440
When we get in with the specialist,

00:46:22.440 --> 00:46:25.070
basically the first thing he told us was

00:46:25.070 --> 00:46:26.550
these are where her curves are.

00:46:26.550 --> 00:46:28.610
This is what this means,
these are the facts,

00:46:28.610 --> 00:46:30.610
this is what research has taught us,

00:46:30.610 --> 00:46:32.327
therefore we are recommending

00:46:32.327 --> 00:46:35.630
spinal fusion surgery for Lily.

00:46:35.630 --> 00:46:38.180
We left the doctor's office, we went out,

00:46:38.180 --> 00:46:40.477
we got in the van and
it was the three of us

00:46:40.477 --> 00:46:43.050
and we talked about it some
more and we cried, you know,

00:46:43.050 --> 00:46:44.300
all three of us cried.

00:46:44.300 --> 00:46:46.790
Of course it was and it is very scary.

00:46:46.790 --> 00:46:51.790
Yeah so that was I believe
before she went in for surgery

00:46:52.070 --> 00:46:57.070
and then episode 95, The
World's Longest Treasure Hunt,

00:46:57.670 --> 00:46:59.780
where Kevin came on
and told us about that.

00:46:59.780 --> 00:47:02.620
Totally different format
we tried for a little bit.

00:47:02.620 --> 00:47:07.070
As part of the intro I gave
an update on Lily's recovery.

00:47:07.070 --> 00:47:10.840
We told her your back's
straight and she started crying.

00:47:10.840 --> 00:47:14.850
The surgery went exactly how we hoped.

00:47:14.850 --> 00:47:17.270
It went off without a hitch.

00:47:17.270 --> 00:47:20.320
It came full circle when we
got in the mini van again,

00:47:20.320 --> 00:47:22.215
leaving the hospital for the first time

00:47:22.215 --> 00:47:26.963
and we all looked at each
other and Lily was like,

00:47:27.980 --> 00:47:31.143
we did it, now she's at home recovering.

00:47:31.990 --> 00:47:34.390
She's doing great.

00:47:34.390 --> 00:47:36.100
But the fact that they went in there

00:47:36.100 --> 00:47:39.070
and they put two metal
rods that they custom-bent.

00:47:39.070 --> 00:47:42.860
They smooshed her spine
to be back no longer an S

00:47:42.860 --> 00:47:47.220
and then they attached those
rods using titanium screws,

00:47:47.220 --> 00:47:51.320
huge screws into her vertebrae.

00:47:51.320 --> 00:47:52.830
You know how many people--
And then six weeks

00:47:52.830 --> 00:47:55.470
after that she's fully
recovered basically.

00:47:55.470 --> 00:47:56.990
But here's the thing.

00:47:56.990 --> 00:47:57.903
Here's the thing what?

00:47:59.930 --> 00:48:01.453
You said here's the thing.

00:48:01.453 --> 00:48:04.403
You got a little insight
into, somebody do a montage

00:48:05.400 --> 00:48:07.680
of me trying to say something.

00:48:07.680 --> 00:48:09.543
Insert something into a Link story.

00:48:10.630 --> 00:48:11.693
Kiki, you do that.

00:48:16.000 --> 00:48:17.100
I thought you were talking to Kiko,

00:48:17.100 --> 00:48:18.930
I was like why'd you call Kiko Kiki?

00:48:18.930 --> 00:48:19.970
I understand now.

00:48:19.970 --> 00:48:20.803
Kiki.

00:48:20.803 --> 00:48:21.653
Not Kiko, Kiki.

00:48:22.530 --> 00:48:24.800
Maybe that goes both ways, I don't know.

00:48:24.800 --> 00:48:26.313
That's a good analysis.

00:48:28.000 --> 00:48:31.700
So that was after we
had made the decision,

00:48:31.700 --> 00:48:36.370
this was well into us making the decision

00:48:36.370 --> 00:48:40.390
for the show to just be the two of us.

00:48:40.390 --> 00:48:42.360
Yeah, yeah.

00:48:42.360 --> 00:48:44.760
'Cause I don't remember
exactly when that was,

00:48:45.690 --> 00:48:50.690
but yeah, well we started
saying that the thing

00:48:51.110 --> 00:48:53.510
that's gonna drive the majority of--

00:48:53.510 --> 00:48:55.970
March of 2017 is when we started.

00:48:55.970 --> 00:48:58.410
Okay so this is very soon after that,

00:48:58.410 --> 00:48:59.690
'cause we were like let's use--

00:48:59.690 --> 00:49:00.760
No more interviews.

00:49:00.760 --> 00:49:04.290
The thing, the stuff that happens to us

00:49:05.439 --> 00:49:09.560
is going to be the majority of the fuel

00:49:09.560 --> 00:49:11.100
for the conversations that we have.

00:49:11.100 --> 00:49:11.933
Right.

00:49:13.864 --> 00:49:15.980
We've talked about this,
many people have made

00:49:15.980 --> 00:49:20.240
the same observation that
some of the personal stories

00:49:20.240 --> 00:49:23.553
that used to be the backbone,
definitely the backbone

00:49:23.553 --> 00:49:26.860
of Good Morning Chia Lincoln,
the predecessor to GMM

00:49:26.860 --> 00:49:29.430
and then the early stages
of Good Mythical Morning,

00:49:29.430 --> 00:49:34.140
very much driven by us having
conversations together.

00:49:34.140 --> 00:49:36.680
That's where let's talk about
that and moving at the speed

00:49:36.680 --> 00:49:39.920
of conversation, a lot of that
was actually originally GMM.

00:49:39.920 --> 00:49:41.330
Yeah.
Mode.

00:49:41.330 --> 00:49:43.810
And of course, for reasons that most

00:49:43.810 --> 00:49:48.010
of you probably understand,
GMM has necessarily taken

00:49:48.010 --> 00:49:50.430
on a different tone and a different angle

00:49:50.430 --> 00:49:52.490
and that's why it's in its 16th,

00:49:52.490 --> 00:49:54.290
it's about to be in its 16th season.

00:49:55.953 --> 00:49:58.773
Because we made that
change and then we took,

00:49:59.850 --> 00:50:03.473
we took that spirit and we
moved it to Ear Biscuits.

00:50:04.720 --> 00:50:06.363
And I think that that was,

00:50:07.270 --> 00:50:11.100
even from a personal health standpoint,

00:50:11.100 --> 00:50:15.113
was a very important transition.

00:50:17.350 --> 00:50:22.228
Because GMM is like a
playground for our personalities

00:50:22.228 --> 00:50:26.780
in which we do things that
grown men probably shouldn't do

00:50:26.780 --> 00:50:30.040
or if they're gonna do it you
might as well watch 'em do it.

00:50:30.040 --> 00:50:34.442
And we have fun but it's
not about being personal

00:50:34.442 --> 00:50:38.979
anymore than just hey,
this is us realistically

00:50:38.979 --> 00:50:41.910
reacting to something in the
moment and we kinda have,

00:50:41.910 --> 00:50:43.810
two best friends having a good time

00:50:43.810 --> 00:50:47.060
but it's not two best friends
pulling back the layers

00:50:47.060 --> 00:50:49.370
of their lives, that's not what GMM is

00:50:49.370 --> 00:50:51.373
and GMM's not ever gonna be that again.

00:50:52.620 --> 00:50:54.860
But we do have this, we
have this place that--

00:50:54.860 --> 00:50:57.847
And GMM was never--
It was never that.

00:50:57.847 --> 00:51:00.428
It was never a venue
to be as introspective

00:51:00.428 --> 00:51:05.420
or everything we did was for
the effect of entertainment.

00:51:05.420 --> 00:51:06.850
We would tell personal stories.

00:51:06.850 --> 00:51:08.340
Funny stories.
We would tell

00:51:08.340 --> 00:51:12.215
the funny stories and
I don't know how much

00:51:12.215 --> 00:51:16.370
we talked about Ear Biscuits
becoming a place where,

00:51:16.370 --> 00:51:17.500
I think the assumption was,

00:51:17.500 --> 00:51:20.149
hey we can share some funny
stories when they happen,

00:51:20.149 --> 00:51:22.610
at least at the top of
an episode before we get

00:51:22.610 --> 00:51:25.220
into something topical, which we still do

00:51:25.220 --> 00:51:27.360
on many of these episodes,

00:51:27.360 --> 00:51:29.770
we kinda use that format when we're trying

00:51:29.770 --> 00:51:32.070
to figure out what we're
gonna make an episode about.

00:51:32.070 --> 00:51:35.707
But then we also expanded
it to whatever's going on,

00:51:35.707 --> 00:51:37.700
when the thing with Lily
happened that might have been

00:51:37.700 --> 00:51:40.200
one of the first things that
I was like, you know what,

00:51:40.200 --> 00:51:42.490
I think I wanna talk about this.

00:51:42.490 --> 00:51:44.740
And it was, at the beginning of that,

00:51:44.740 --> 00:51:48.390
the first Lily clip it was
like, speaking of Lily,

00:51:48.390 --> 00:51:51.883
and then I talked about what was going on.

00:51:54.076 --> 00:51:56.530
But it wasn't the entire episode and then

00:51:56.530 --> 00:51:58.610
for the Kevin thing, it
was like hey since I talked

00:51:58.610 --> 00:52:00.560
about Lily I wanna give you an update

00:52:00.560 --> 00:52:02.900
on that subsequent clip we played.

00:52:02.900 --> 00:52:04.433
Right.
But it was,

00:52:05.850 --> 00:52:09.510
it felt good to be able to
share and for us to process

00:52:09.510 --> 00:52:14.510
as friends something
that was such a big part

00:52:14.580 --> 00:52:17.959
of my life, you know, and
that's when I felt like

00:52:17.959 --> 00:52:22.959
that level of honesty and
introspection was rewarding.

00:52:25.952 --> 00:52:28.673
Just for us to be able to talk about it,

00:52:28.673 --> 00:52:32.915
without even knowing how a
listener would respond to it

00:52:32.915 --> 00:52:35.393
and maybe that's part
of what you're saying,

00:52:35.393 --> 00:52:37.390
the more that we're
able to have those type

00:52:37.390 --> 00:52:42.390
of conversations, it's difficult
for us to find the time

00:52:42.930 --> 00:52:45.833
to have the conversations that,

00:52:46.990 --> 00:52:50.370
where we're sharing things
about our experience.

00:52:50.370 --> 00:52:53.430
When the, hits the fan, we
absolutely talk about things,

00:52:53.430 --> 00:52:56.610
we don't wait to do that on the podcast.

00:52:56.610 --> 00:52:58.053
That would be ridiculous.

00:52:58.980 --> 00:53:00.130
So I'm not saying that.

00:53:00.997 --> 00:53:02.320
For instance--
In addition to that--

00:53:02.320 --> 00:53:04.580
It wasn't like I didn't know--

00:53:04.580 --> 00:53:06.130
Right.
The update with Lily,

00:53:06.130 --> 00:53:10.510
but I might not know what
you did on your vacation.

00:53:10.510 --> 00:53:13.000
The funny story that you're
gonna tell about your RV

00:53:13.000 --> 00:53:14.490
trying to get out of the driveway, well,

00:53:14.490 --> 00:53:16.160
you're not gonna tell that to me.

00:53:16.160 --> 00:53:19.071
In the same way.
Because you don't need to.

00:53:19.071 --> 00:53:20.520
That can wait for the podcast.

00:53:20.520 --> 00:53:23.650
And that's the Good
Morning Chia Lincoln part

00:53:23.650 --> 00:53:25.170
of it was I'm gonna tell
you this funny story

00:53:25.170 --> 00:53:27.730
about the RV, we should put
that on the internet too.

00:53:27.730 --> 00:53:31.170
Two for one, but then
we're both gonna talk about

00:53:32.123 --> 00:53:37.123
what these vacations mean
to us as humans, husbands,

00:53:38.030 --> 00:53:42.390
fathers, you know, pet
owners, all of those things.

00:53:42.390 --> 00:53:46.730
So the show started to,
it evolved, but it started

00:53:46.730 --> 00:53:51.210
to morph a little bit and I
should just give a quick update

00:53:51.210 --> 00:53:53.770
on Lily since we've talked about her.

00:53:53.770 --> 00:53:55.920
It's been over two years.

00:53:55.920 --> 00:53:59.440
We celebrated the two year
anniversary of her surgery

00:53:59.440 --> 00:54:03.630
and she had been rollerskating
recently and somebody came up

00:54:03.630 --> 00:54:05.713
and clipped her from behind
and knocked her on her butt

00:54:05.713 --> 00:54:09.340
and then she fell on her back
and it was the first time

00:54:09.340 --> 00:54:10.450
that we were actually scared.

00:54:10.450 --> 00:54:11.830
Did everything just pop out?

00:54:11.830 --> 00:54:14.919
Yeah, her whole spine--
Like Mortal Kombat.

00:54:17.080 --> 00:54:20.690
No, she had some bruised ribs but nothing,

00:54:20.690 --> 00:54:25.690
it just so happened
that her annual check-up

00:54:25.956 --> 00:54:29.440
with the spine doctor was within the week.

00:54:29.440 --> 00:54:32.960
So that put our minds at
rest when they do the x-ray

00:54:32.960 --> 00:54:35.060
and say you know what,
everything is totally fine.

00:54:35.060 --> 00:54:37.090
It's all metal-infused.

00:54:37.090 --> 00:54:38.730
After two years--
It's stronger than

00:54:38.730 --> 00:54:40.250
a normal spine.
Well the screws

00:54:40.250 --> 00:54:43.868
that they used to put the rods
in, by this point in time,

00:54:43.868 --> 00:54:46.950
it's like, it's totally become one.

00:54:46.950 --> 00:54:47.930
Right, it's all grown together.

00:54:47.930 --> 00:54:50.700
So actually in the first year or so,

00:54:50.700 --> 00:54:53.582
there is a chance--
Of Mortal Kombat.

00:54:53.582 --> 00:54:55.721
No, of the screws getting loose.

00:54:55.721 --> 00:54:57.220
Right.
And them having

00:54:57.220 --> 00:55:00.690
to do something, so we've
passed that milestone.

00:55:00.690 --> 00:55:02.390
Her number are check-ups are going down

00:55:02.390 --> 00:55:04.840
to where they don't have
to be every year anymore,

00:55:04.840 --> 00:55:09.840
so it was a miraculous surgery
and we're grateful for it,

00:55:11.730 --> 00:55:16.730
she's doing fabulous and she
has no physical restrictions.

00:55:17.200 --> 00:55:21.180
And she has the scar that
still, she wears as a badge

00:55:21.180 --> 00:55:24.193
of honor and it's like, when she has one

00:55:24.193 --> 00:55:27.310
of the shorter shirts that
these girls are wearing now

00:55:27.310 --> 00:55:29.020
where it's like you can see the belt,

00:55:29.020 --> 00:55:31.568
I wouldn't call it a midriff
but if you bend over,

00:55:31.568 --> 00:55:34.914
you can see a little bit
of the scar and it's like

00:55:34.914 --> 00:55:37.620
I'm proud every time
I see it and I'm proud

00:55:37.620 --> 00:55:42.530
that she's fine with a bathing suit on,

00:55:42.530 --> 00:55:44.360
hanging out with her friends.

00:55:44.360 --> 00:55:46.060
It's not anything she's embarrassed about

00:55:46.060 --> 00:55:48.892
and she's had many conversations
over the past two years

00:55:48.892 --> 00:55:53.892
with other people who are
faced with the same surgery

00:55:54.970 --> 00:55:57.400
or a similar condition
and she's able to see

00:55:57.400 --> 00:56:01.910
how she can, her experience
can enrich the lives

00:56:01.910 --> 00:56:03.690
of other people and give them support,

00:56:03.690 --> 00:56:08.690
so it was scary, it was
life-changing for all of us

00:56:09.650 --> 00:56:10.973
and it continues to be.

00:56:12.210 --> 00:56:16.360
I think, I have a...

00:56:18.030 --> 00:56:21.320
I have a personal...

00:56:23.560 --> 00:56:27.310
I'm annoyed by sentimentality porn.

00:56:27.310 --> 00:56:29.863
Okay, I'll use, for lack of a better word.

00:56:31.070 --> 00:56:35.690
Meaning I'm going to say
something sentimental

00:56:35.690 --> 00:56:37.090
or I'm gonna do something

00:56:37.090 --> 00:56:38.810
or I'm gonna share something sentimental

00:56:38.810 --> 00:56:42.380
because I know that it
will affect the audience

00:56:42.380 --> 00:56:46.010
in a certain way, and it's manipulation.

00:56:46.010 --> 00:56:48.990
It's a manipulation thing and so I think

00:56:48.990 --> 00:56:53.990
that there are definitely,
when you talk about vlogging,

00:56:54.236 --> 00:56:57.930
personal vlogs where people
are like I've made a decision

00:56:57.930 --> 00:57:00.290
that I'm gonna share my life with you,

00:57:00.290 --> 00:57:03.879
I think you get into a
place where you start hoping

00:57:03.879 --> 00:57:06.310
that you're going to
experience things that then

00:57:06.310 --> 00:57:10.585
will make deep, moving material

00:57:10.585 --> 00:57:13.550
that just grows your audience, right?

00:57:13.550 --> 00:57:16.453
And I just wanna be clear
that that's not what we do

00:57:16.453 --> 00:57:18.500
on Ear Biscuits.

00:57:18.500 --> 00:57:21.970
I think that's probably
clear but that's one

00:57:21.970 --> 00:57:25.810
of the reasons that we
don't only format the show

00:57:25.810 --> 00:57:27.410
to be about personal things.

00:57:27.410 --> 00:57:31.780
Because if we said it's always
personal every single time,

00:57:31.780 --> 00:57:34.130
it's gonna be about what's
going on in our lives,

00:57:34.130 --> 00:57:36.110
then you begin to script
your life for the sake

00:57:36.110 --> 00:57:37.470
of the content that you're creating.

00:57:37.470 --> 00:57:38.303
Yeah.
I think that's one

00:57:38.303 --> 00:57:41.170
of the reasons that and
I don't have anything

00:57:41.170 --> 00:57:45.835
against people who have
daily vlogs or family vlogs

00:57:45.835 --> 00:57:48.750
but I would be lying
to you if I didn't say

00:57:48.750 --> 00:57:52.490
that almost all of them
without exception annoy me.

00:57:52.490 --> 00:57:57.150
And sort of, I think one of
the things that annoys me

00:57:57.150 --> 00:58:00.810
about it is it's just like
your family has become a show.

00:58:00.810 --> 00:58:05.150
And at that point, you
begin to make decisions

00:58:05.150 --> 00:58:07.680
where the content is driving
the way you live your life

00:58:07.680 --> 00:58:09.130
as opposed to the way you live your life

00:58:09.130 --> 00:58:10.333
just being the content.

00:58:11.240 --> 00:58:13.851
Listen, I understand, I can only imagine

00:58:13.851 --> 00:58:17.990
what it would feel like to
be in that kind of cycle

00:58:17.990 --> 00:58:20.210
and I'm glad that I'm not in that cycle

00:58:20.210 --> 00:58:25.210
but with Ear Biscuits, it's
like if something happens,

00:58:25.837 --> 00:58:30.837
and I think that just
statistically or circumstantially,

00:58:31.500 --> 00:58:36.310
whatever, I haven't had
this situation happen with,

00:58:36.310 --> 00:58:38.140
I haven't had that happen with my kid.

00:58:38.140 --> 00:58:40.720
I haven't had a medical
situation with my kid.

00:58:40.720 --> 00:58:43.094
I haven't had a close relative die

00:58:43.094 --> 00:58:44.730
like your grandfather died.

00:58:44.730 --> 00:58:48.697
I haven't had my mom's husband
who incidentally is my dad.

00:58:48.697 --> 00:58:52.770
Hasn't been through a difficult time

00:58:52.770 --> 00:58:54.343
in the hospital so Link has
shared a lot of those things

00:58:54.343 --> 00:58:58.073
so I'm not saying Link is
doing sentimentality porn,

00:58:58.073 --> 00:59:00.799
I'm just saying that as things happen,

00:59:00.799 --> 00:59:02.650
if we feel that they're appropriate,

00:59:02.650 --> 00:59:03.931
we're gonna talk about them

00:59:03.931 --> 00:59:05.800
and we're gonna process them together

00:59:05.800 --> 00:59:10.370
because we're purposely
choosing to format the episodes

00:59:10.370 --> 00:59:14.290
in a way that gives us the
ability to share personal things

00:59:14.290 --> 00:59:16.755
but doesn't make the point--
An obligation.

00:59:16.755 --> 00:59:18.840
Of the podcast to be
sharing personal things

00:59:18.840 --> 00:59:22.560
at which point I feel
like we would be hoping

00:59:22.560 --> 00:59:25.620
that things would happen
or sensationalizing things

00:59:25.620 --> 00:59:28.726
that are happening to us
for your entertainment.

00:59:28.726 --> 00:59:32.035
And again, this is a business.

00:59:32.035 --> 00:59:36.756
We do this in part so that we
can get money from sponsors,

00:59:36.756 --> 00:59:39.493
that we can grow our business
at Mythical Entertainment,

00:59:39.493 --> 00:59:42.910
that we would be dishonest
if we weren't transparent

00:59:42.910 --> 00:59:46.430
about that but I think more than that,

00:59:46.430 --> 00:59:50.680
it has become a place for
us to process the things

00:59:50.680 --> 00:59:53.743
that we are experiencing and also,

00:59:55.464 --> 00:59:58.720
just be like hey, we're gonna
be open about our lives,

00:59:58.720 --> 01:00:00.940
we're gonna be open about
what it's like to be,

01:00:00.940 --> 01:00:03.020
to run a business, to be a best friend,

01:00:03.020 --> 01:00:05.080
to be a husband, to be a father.

01:00:05.080 --> 01:00:06.490
As these things happen to us,

01:00:06.490 --> 01:00:09.430
we're gonna try to accurately portray them

01:00:09.430 --> 01:00:11.460
and just share our experience.

01:00:11.460 --> 01:00:13.950
And I've really tried to understand what

01:00:13.950 --> 01:00:17.430
my personal boundaries are for sharing

01:00:17.430 --> 01:00:19.800
and what the objective of that is.

01:00:19.800 --> 01:00:24.307
And I will say, I will
also acknowledge that yes,

01:00:26.020 --> 01:00:29.213
you haven't had things happen in your life

01:00:29.213 --> 01:00:31.980
since we've been sharing this
type of stuff on Ear Biscuits

01:00:31.980 --> 01:00:36.980
that have had this headline
material sensational type thing

01:00:37.040 --> 01:00:40.297
like the stuff that you
listed that I've shared about.

01:00:40.297 --> 01:00:43.474
But that's not to say, I mean,

01:00:43.474 --> 01:00:45.450
I couldn't have predicted
that I would have been

01:00:45.450 --> 01:00:46.990
sharing those things.

01:00:46.990 --> 01:00:48.137
If you would have told
me I would have said,

01:00:48.137 --> 01:00:51.260
"I can believe you but I don't
know how we'll get there."

01:00:51.260 --> 01:00:55.018
I don't know that you would have predicted

01:00:55.018 --> 01:00:57.452
that you would be sharing
openly about therapy

01:00:57.452 --> 01:01:00.180
and the things that you're
learning but I think

01:01:00.180 --> 01:01:02.880
that it's to your credit, I think it's...

01:01:05.290 --> 01:01:07.240
You're also doing that.

01:01:07.240 --> 01:01:09.440
We're honestly processing our lives

01:01:09.440 --> 01:01:11.600
and that's what this show has become.

01:01:11.600 --> 01:01:15.240
It's processing our lives
and our perspectives

01:01:16.798 --> 01:01:20.950
but I 100% agree that it's
not with an obligation

01:01:20.950 --> 01:01:24.060
to over share especially to the point

01:01:24.060 --> 01:01:26.203
of sensationalizing things

01:01:26.203 --> 01:01:28.870
or hoping that something
sensational happens.

01:01:28.870 --> 01:01:29.790
Right.

01:01:29.790 --> 01:01:32.343
But in terms of my boundary,

01:01:34.262 --> 01:01:36.650
even though we've done episodes

01:01:36.650 --> 01:01:38.480
where they were advice-themed,

01:01:38.480 --> 01:01:43.060
I never want to turn my
personal experience back

01:01:43.060 --> 01:01:45.620
at the audience as advice.

01:01:45.620 --> 01:01:48.753
I just wanna honestly process and then,

01:01:51.719 --> 01:01:56.719
if I'm honest, then you
as a listener can take

01:01:58.285 --> 01:02:01.410
whatever's valuable to
you, that's up to you,

01:02:01.410 --> 01:02:06.410
but I try to be disciplined
to not turn my experience

01:02:07.890 --> 01:02:11.630
into advice for somebody else even though

01:02:11.630 --> 01:02:13.100
I do like givin' advice

01:02:13.100 --> 01:02:15.453
and I like feelin' like
I'm right about stuff.

01:02:17.278 --> 01:02:22.090
But I'm very grateful with the way

01:02:22.090 --> 01:02:23.640
that Ear Biscuits has expanded.

01:02:23.640 --> 01:02:28.410
I think that again, this
is not the primary venue

01:02:28.410 --> 01:02:32.150
for us to have the deepest,
deepest conversations

01:02:32.150 --> 01:02:36.110
that we have, but it's that middle ground

01:02:36.110 --> 01:02:41.110
of conversation, being
able to tell the story

01:02:41.130 --> 01:02:43.990
for comedic effect and us
to experience that together

01:02:43.990 --> 01:02:46.140
is really fun, that's really
great for our relationship.

01:02:46.140 --> 01:02:46.973
Yeah.

01:02:46.973 --> 01:02:51.890
And also, when we get into
the introspective aspects of,

01:02:51.890 --> 01:02:53.960
I told you the RV story, that was funny,

01:02:53.960 --> 01:02:55.750
but then we're gonna
talk about our vacations

01:02:55.750 --> 01:02:58.313
and I think we do get at things that,

01:02:58.313 --> 01:03:02.103
it's that middle ground of
conversation that's kinda like,

01:03:04.040 --> 01:03:07.120
you find yourself being
introspective at a point

01:03:07.120 --> 01:03:08.850
where we probably would have just moved

01:03:08.850 --> 01:03:11.440
onto working otherwise.
Right.

01:03:11.440 --> 01:03:16.440
So I do think that I'm very happy with

01:03:17.038 --> 01:03:21.030
where Ear Biscuits is for
that reason and then I'll say,

01:03:21.030 --> 01:03:24.100
secondarily, I've been very encouraged,

01:03:26.920 --> 01:03:31.920
pleasantly surprised that the
more that we have been honest

01:03:32.420 --> 01:03:35.163
about our perspectives
and our experiences,

01:03:35.163 --> 01:03:38.440
that listeners have related to it

01:03:38.440 --> 01:03:42.173
and that it's helped them in some way.

01:03:43.722 --> 01:03:47.870
Yeah, it's been one of the
most encouraging things

01:03:49.612 --> 01:03:53.120
from people that we meet on tour.

01:03:53.120 --> 01:03:53.953
Yeah.

01:03:53.953 --> 01:03:56.140
And of course there's lots
of people who are like

01:03:56.140 --> 01:03:57.900
the bright spot that GMM represents

01:03:57.900 --> 01:04:01.020
in my day has been
transformative for me emotionally

01:04:01.020 --> 01:04:03.400
and again, we never
intended that but the fact

01:04:03.400 --> 01:04:06.803
that that's a reality is just
something that blows us away,

01:04:07.657 --> 01:04:12.570
but I think that when somebody
says Ear Biscuits has...

01:04:15.440 --> 01:04:17.880
This particular thing
that you talked about

01:04:19.530 --> 01:04:21.850
changed the way that I approach this thing

01:04:21.850 --> 01:04:23.830
or thought about this thing in my life.

01:04:23.830 --> 01:04:26.030
Again, not from a hey
we're giving you advice.

01:04:26.030 --> 01:04:30.710
Usually the advice that we give
is for some stupid situation

01:04:30.710 --> 01:04:32.410
and for the sake of entertainment.

01:04:33.562 --> 01:04:36.860
But just, when you hear
somebody talk about something

01:04:36.860 --> 01:04:40.460
that there's a touch point in your life

01:04:40.460 --> 01:04:42.864
and you're seeing how
they're navigating it,

01:04:42.864 --> 01:04:46.710
that's just how humans relate
to each other, you know.

01:04:46.710 --> 01:04:49.470
It's not like hey look
at me and do what we did,

01:04:49.470 --> 01:04:50.560
but it's just like, hey,

01:04:50.560 --> 01:04:52.480
I'm just gonna share honestly about this.

01:04:52.480 --> 01:04:55.450
Any relationship and I do
think that it's not just

01:04:55.450 --> 01:04:56.673
the relationship that
we have with each other,

01:04:56.673 --> 01:04:59.423
it's the relationship that
we have with you listening,

01:05:00.540 --> 01:05:03.710
anytime that you're just
open about something

01:05:03.710 --> 01:05:07.791
that's going on, that enhances,

01:05:07.791 --> 01:05:10.520
there's the opportunity for
that to enhance your life

01:05:10.520 --> 01:05:15.203
and that's a responsibility
I think that we didn't design

01:05:15.203 --> 01:05:20.203
the show to ever have
but I think it does now

01:05:20.300 --> 01:05:24.220
and so we think about that,
when we're sharing something

01:05:24.220 --> 01:05:27.770
that we're going through,
I think we're very,

01:05:27.770 --> 01:05:30.090
very much aware that there
are individuals out there

01:05:30.090 --> 01:05:33.210
who are going through something
very similar potentially

01:05:33.210 --> 01:05:35.280
or will go through something similar

01:05:35.280 --> 01:05:38.893
and if that can improve their experience,

01:05:39.763 --> 01:05:43.450
then that alone makes
this whole thing worth it.

01:05:43.450 --> 01:05:45.499
And from a content standpoint,

01:05:45.499 --> 01:05:47.920
just to get a little more practical,

01:05:47.920 --> 01:05:50.250
I'm very encouraged when I see,

01:05:50.250 --> 01:05:52.650
well my heart sinks a
little bit when I look at,

01:05:53.630 --> 01:05:55.360
if I see the Good Mythical Morning

01:05:55.360 --> 01:05:57.050
or the Rhett and Link Reddit thread

01:05:57.050 --> 01:06:02.050
and a new entry will pop
up where somebody says,

01:06:03.060 --> 01:06:06.160
I miss the old Good Mythical Morning

01:06:06.160 --> 01:06:09.210
or this is what I don't like
about Good Mythical Morning

01:06:09.210 --> 01:06:12.940
or I miss Good Morning Chia
Lincoln and then inevitably,

01:06:12.940 --> 01:06:16.503
the conversation very quickly turns to,

01:06:17.390 --> 01:06:20.007
it sounds like Ear Biscuits
is perfect for you.

01:06:20.007 --> 01:06:21.140
You know?
Yeah.

01:06:21.140 --> 01:06:22.530
So I'm--
Yeah.

01:06:22.530 --> 01:06:24.926
It's funny that, I don't know
how much we talked about it

01:06:24.926 --> 01:06:27.507
and I don't know how intentional it was

01:06:27.507 --> 01:06:31.760
but as Good Mythical Morning has evolved,

01:06:31.760 --> 01:06:36.680
then Ear Biscuits also
evolved to fill a desire

01:06:36.680 --> 01:06:41.680
for something that no longer
is the achieved dynamic

01:06:41.760 --> 01:06:45.324
of that show which is just
unstructured conversation

01:06:45.324 --> 01:06:50.324
that gets to personal
stories that are funny

01:06:50.960 --> 01:06:55.040
and as we've discussed, it
goes a lot further than that.

01:06:57.270 --> 01:07:02.270
I'm just, I'm glad to be
200 episodes into something

01:07:03.260 --> 01:07:05.880
that we didn't get complacent,

01:07:05.880 --> 01:07:10.880
but we continued to want to
express ourselves creatively

01:07:12.240 --> 01:07:16.500
and to find a way to be engaged.

01:07:16.500 --> 01:07:18.050
'Cause I think that anything that--

01:07:18.050 --> 01:07:19.610
And then this is what happened.

01:07:19.610 --> 01:07:24.540
You anticipate going for
an extended period of time.

01:07:24.540 --> 01:07:29.274
As a performer, if
you're creating a product

01:07:29.274 --> 01:07:32.663
that does require you to
perform on some level,

01:07:34.738 --> 01:07:38.210
believe it or not, we are
performing in one sense

01:07:38.210 --> 01:07:39.630
because we're not just
talking to each other,

01:07:39.630 --> 01:07:41.970
we're having a conversation
in this context

01:07:41.970 --> 01:07:43.120
where you're listening.

01:07:43.960 --> 01:07:48.830
Anytime you can transform
that into something that

01:07:49.893 --> 01:07:52.963
does not feel like a burden,

01:07:54.172 --> 01:07:56.070
I think you're in a good place,

01:07:56.070 --> 01:07:57.930
and not only does this
not feel like a burden,

01:07:57.930 --> 01:08:00.062
but this feels like an outlet for us.

01:08:00.062 --> 01:08:01.231
Yeah.

01:08:01.231 --> 01:08:05.270
And I gotta be honest,
with the interviews,

01:08:05.270 --> 01:08:06.960
